<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <!--
    The mark's one colour, and the one dictionary in this folder that is deliberately not a
    palette.

    Every key in Light.xaml exists in Dark.xaml, because a themed surface has to repaint when the
    palette is swapped. This one must not. The same mark is drawn in the title bar and on the
    taskbar by Windows, out of an icon file that cannot switch with anything; a rail mark that
    followed the theme would agree with the window it sits in and disagree with the icon two
    inches above it.

    MarkInk carries the same value as the dark palette's AccentFill and is kept apart from it on
    purpose: sharing the key would let a palette decision repaint the product's mark. It is the
    one colour in the palette that clears 3:1 against a light taskbar and a dark one alike -
    3.72 and 3.95 - which is why the mark wears it on both rather than having a version for each.
    The light palette's Accent gives 2.87 on a dark taskbar and cannot be used here.

    One key and not two: the mark is a single ink on nothing. It had a tile and a knocked-out
    glyph until the owner chose a tile-less one, and a MarkGlyph naming a colour the mark does
    not contain would be a key that lies.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MarkInk" Color="#2D7FD3" />
</ResourceDictionary>
